,{"kind":"content","content":[{"level":2,"type":"heading","text":"Discussion","anchor":"discussion"},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"Every pair’s minimum cut value is the least capacity on its tree path, and every tree edge"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"splits the vertices into a minimum cut between its ends. Computed by Gusfield’s algorithm"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"with the canonical cut, vertices in order, rooted at the first: n − 1 maximum flows on one"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"residual network, by push–relabel’s first phase. Disconnected graphs get zero-capacity tree"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"edges. "},{"code":"capacity","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":" is called once per non-loop edge, in position order, before any work."}]},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"The flows run in a wider type than "},{"code":"C","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":" where needed ("},{"code":"Int","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":" or "},{"code":"Int128","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":" for fixed-width"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"integers, "},{"code":"Double","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":" for "},{"code":"Float","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":"), so a vertex’s capacity sum may exceed "},{"code":"C","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":"’s range."}]},{"style":"note","type":"aside","name":"Precondition","content":[{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"text":"Every capacity is at least zero, not NaN and finite, and every tree","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"capacity (a minimum cut value) fits in ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"C"},{"text":".","type":"text"}]}]}]}],"metadata":{"externalID":"s:14GraphProtocols0A0P5FlowsE12gomoryHuTree8capacityAD06GomoryeF0Vyxqd__GSgqd__5Edges_5IndexQZXE_ts18AdditiveArithmeticRd__SL